<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Thames</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Moraviantown · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Thames was fought on 5 October 1813 near Moraviantown in Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. An American army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-harrison">William Henry Harrison</a> defeated a retreating British and Native force under Henry Procter and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>, who was killed. The battle restored American control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a> frontier and ended Tecumseh's confederacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-thames.jpg" alt="Battle of the Thames" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Emmons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_the_Thames.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>British and Indigenous forces under Major General Isaac Brock and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a> had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a> in August 1812, and in January 1813 Henry Procter's Right Division and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wyandot">Wyandot</a> war leader Roundhead defeated James Winchester's Americans at Frenchtown on the River Raisin. On 10 September 1813 Oliver Hazard Perry captured all six ships of Robert Heriot Barclay's squadron at the Battle of Lake Erie, cutting Procter's supply line and taking much of Fort Amherstburg's artillery, which had been mounted on the ships.</p><p>Without consulting his senior officers, Procter decided to abandon the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a> frontier and withdraw up the Thames valley towards Burlington Heights. Tecumseh, who knew a retreat would end British support for his confederacy, tried in vain to dissuade him. On 27 September, after burning Detroit and Amherstburg, 880 British regulars marched from Sandwich, followed by Tecumseh with 1,200 warriors and their families.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The retreat was slow in rain and on bad roads, and Procter rarely shared his plans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-harrison">William Henry Harrison</a> landed below Amherstburg on 27 September and gave chase on 2 October. By the time he caught up west of Moraviantown on 5 October, half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>'s warriors had drifted away, and Procter had only about 450 regulars and about 500 warriors against roughly 2,500 Americans, many of them Kentucky militia.</p><p>The British formed two lines in open woodland between the river and the Backmetack Marsh, without tools to build defences, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>'s warriors waited in the marsh. Lieutenant Colonel James Johnson's mounted infantry charged shouting Remember the Raisin, broke both lines and took most of the British prisoner; Procter narrowly escaped. In the marsh Richard Mentor Johnson led a forlorn hope of 20 men against Tecumseh's flank, and Tecumseh was killed. His followers held for about 30 minutes before withdrawing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Harrison reported seven killed and 22 wounded and 601 prisoners, counting those taken before and after the battle. He burned Moraviantown and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a>. Tecumseh's alliance dissolved, though some tribes fought on. Johnson, usually credited with killing Tecumseh, later became vice president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. A court martial at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> found Procter erroneous in judgment and deficient in energy, ending his career.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Thames” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tecumseh allied with Britain in the War of 1812 and helped capture Detroit, but he was killed at the <b>Battle of the Thames</b> in October 1813, and the confederacy collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/state/tecumsehs-confederacy">Tecumseh's Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
